## 1. Proposal summary

The minimum product is a local desktop workspace for importing, inspecting, annotating, saving, reopening, and exporting microscopy projects. An image source is opened read-only. Its axes, calibration, metadata bytes, identity, and reader version are kept separate from channel display settings and from derived/resampled data. An annotation references a particular source identity and frame. An export declares its coordinate convention, transform history, and which image values or display mappings it contains.

The architecture is a project directory with immutable save generations, a bounded reader service, one explicit frame/coordinate model, a display controller, an annotation store with undo/redo, and a transactional exporter. The first reader profiles target local TIFF/BigTIFF, OME-TIFF, ImageJ TIFF, and local OME-Zarr 0.4/0.5 sources. That is a candidate support boundary, not a compatibility claim: a profile is enabled only after its specific layout, compression, dtype, axis, calibration, and region-read fixtures pass. Very large strips/pages or vendor arrangements that require broad decoding are rejected for the bounded-review path with an actionable explanation.

