### 5.3 Deliberate support boundary

**[CHOICE]** First fixtures cover local, single-image TIFF/BigTIFF and OME-TIFF with 2–5 supported named axes, one supported sample dtype per fixture family, and uncompressed/LZW/Deflate paths; ImageJ hyperstack support is limited to explicitly fixture-tested axis/metadata layouts. For large-data review, a tiled/chunked layout or a contiguous layout that the reader can memory-map is a candidate. A strip, page, or compressed segment may require broader reads; it is only accepted for the large-data path when measurement shows that its actual decode granularity fits the memory and latency envelope. A 3 GB file is not promised to be bounded merely because it is TIFF. [INFER from SRC-TIFF-README.]

Local OME-Zarr v0.4/0.5 accepts stores only after the matching version/codec/layout fixture passes. Unsupported v1 cases include >5 axes, incompatible or ambiguous axes, non-spatial axes with no user mapping, unsupported codec/sample layout, stitched/whole-slide vendor families outside the tested profile, OJPEG, nonlocal stores, and a dataset whose source geometry cannot be mapped without an unrecorded guess. For these, preserve the original source and metadata, keep a recoverable project record, show why reading or annotation is blocked, and allow a future adapter/import correction. Never rewrite or discard the original to make it fit. Tifffile’s own README lists TIFF limitations and vendor quirks; its broad format list does not remove our need for a small tested subset. [FACT SRC-TIFF-README §§183–272; CHOICE.]

## 6. Persistence, identity, sharing, and resource envelope

### 6.1 Save generations and recovery

Project layout:

```text
project/
  project.json                 # stable project id, schema, display defaults
  CURRENT                      # committed generation id + manifest hash
  CURRENT.prev                 # last committed pointer for recovery
  generations/<generation-id>/ # immutable source registry, annotations, ops, metadata
    manifest.json              # file hashes, schema and same generation id
  imported/                    # only sources the user explicitly copies in
  cache/                       # disposable tiles/previews; outside authority
  exports/                     # separately generated artifacts/manifests
  staging/                     # incomplete import/save/export work
```

**[CHOICE]** A save writes every dirty file to a new generation in a staging directory on the same filesystem. Flush file contents, validate schemas/references/generation IDs and manifest hashes, sync the generation and containing directory where the OS/filesystem exposes those operations, and rename the completed generation into the immutable `generations/` namespace. Then write a temporary `CURRENT` in the project root, flush it, and atomically replace the old `CURRENT`; preserve the prior pointer as `CURRENT.prev` before switching. Every record, manifest, source binding, and export names its generation so a reader cannot accidentally combine files from different saves. Keep at least the current and previous two valid generations.

This uses rename for one pathname only. The captured Linux `rename(2)` documentation says an existing destination can be atomically replaced and `EXDEV` applies across mount points; it also warns NFS clients cannot infer from a failed call that the rename did not happen. [FACT SRC-RENAME §§100–120, 306–311, 370–376.] It does not prove multi-file transactionality, power-loss durability, or NFS/SMB behavior. Before pointer switch, a crash leaves the old generation current and an ignorable staging generation. After pointer switch, open validates the newly named generation; if pointer or manifest is invalid, try the preserved previous pointer and show a recovery report. No automatic “newest timestamp wins” when more than one candidate is valid.

For v1, save in the project’s local working directory; finish and close the project before copying a bundle to ordinary shared storage. Do not support simultaneous editing of a project from several computers. Direct project saves on NFS/SMB remain disabled until kill-at-each-boundary tests pass on that actual filesystem. A failed export writes under a sibling `.partial` name and only replaces the final export after all files/manifests validate. An old export remains available until that publish step.

The base proposal’s earlier order—replace a file and then move the previous good file to backups—could not preserve the just-replaced bytes. The revised design makes previous generations immutable before changing the pointer. The inherited W3 report of local `os.replace` behavior is not evidence for this generation algorithm or a network filesystem.

### 6.2 Source identity and relocation

A regular-file identity is SHA-256 over every source byte plus recorded byte length. A directory-store identity is SHA-256 of a canonical manifest containing every regular file recursively, including metadata and chunks: normalized relative UTF-8 paths, byte lengths, and per-file SHA-256. Sort paths by UTF-8 byte order; serialize canonical JSON as UTF-8 with sorted object keys, compact separators, no trailing newline, no NaN/Infinity. Reject symlinks, special files, invalid/non-normalized paths, path traversal, and normalization collisions in this v1 identity mode. Empty directories carry no bytes and are not identity-bearing.

Hash files by streaming. The source is treated read-only and stable during import/hash. Record the complete manifest. Verify a copy by computing the same manifest after copying. Reopen/relocation uses the same whole-file or whole-tree identity. Size/mtime/path can be used as a quick “possibly changed” check only; it is not identity evidence and cannot authorize silent relocation. Tree hashing can take time, so show cancellable progress. If a mutable store changes during hashing or later review/export, mark drift and block identity-sensitive export until the user resolves it; accepting a new identity marks existing annotations suspect.

This is a candidate format choice, not a supplied standard. **[EXEC W-R02]** checks the small canonical-manifest algorithm for order invariance and content sensitivity; it does not hash an actual OME-Zarr directory or prove filesystem snapshot consistency.

Reference imports store source URI/path and identity; the project remains small but depends on locating the source. Copy imports verify bytes after copy and can relocate with the project at a potentially high storage cost. A `.birw-bundle` archive is called portable only when its bundle manifest says all referenced source bytes are included and their identities verify. A ZIP containing only project JSON while pointing to external 3 GB files is an exchangeable project bundle with external requirements, not a self-contained portable bundle. Bundle manifests list external paths and resolution steps explicitly.

### 6.3 Bounded reads and failure handling

**[INFER]** The 3 GB target requires bounded region reads, a byte-budgeted LRU tile/chunk cache (initial candidate 512 MiB, configurable), viewport-only prefetch, and a limit on concurrent decode work. No default import makes a second full source copy. Mmap is only an option for supported contiguous files and is not treated as proof of low resident memory. Tiled/chunked layouts, strips/pages, compression, pyramid levels, sample type, and storage latency each get separate benchmarks. Show operation progress and a cancel action; cancellation stops queued/decode work promptly and leaves staging artifacts removable on next open.

**[VALIDATION V-R1, UNEXECUTED]** On representative 3 GB tiled, chunked, contiguous/mmap, and strip/page files on an 8-core/16 GB workstation: p95 viewport-tile latency under 100 ms at a fixed review zoom, peak process RSS under 4 GB, cancellation response under 250 ms, no second full decoded array, and no original-file byte changes. The thresholds are proposed product targets, not facts established by tifffile or napari. If one layout misses the gate, that layout is excluded from large-data mode while smaller-file support may remain.

## 7. Derived data, exports, and validation plan

Every derived image operation records source ID and identity, output ID and identity, input/output frames, matrix, resampling method, interpolation, boundaries, dtype/sample conversion, and tool/dependency versions. A channel composite or window/level screenshot is a rendered derivative. A geometric transform is a data operation. The UI and manifests use those terms consistently.

The export manifest records: project and generation IDs; source ID and exact identity scope/hash; frame ID and matrix; coordinate and sample-center convention; axis order/adapter; selectors and annotation scope; annotation IDs and styles; operations applied; output frame/dimensions/dtype; interpolation; channel/window mapping baked into a raster, if any; losses/conversions; output hashes; and unresolved calibration flags. On source drift the default is to refuse an identity-sensitive export and explain how to retain the old unresolved state or explicitly accept a new identity with annotations marked suspect.

